##DESCRIPTION
## Hooke's law; relating force to displacement, calculating elastic potential ## energy
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillations)
## DBsection(Hooke's law)
## Date(26 December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Santo D'Agostino)
## TitleText('')
## AuthorText('')
## EditionText('2018')
## Section('16.1')
## Problem('007')
## KEYWORDS(Hooke's law, mechanics, spring, Newton's second law)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$m  = random(1.1, 2.9, 0.1);
$g  = 9.8;
$x  = 0.025;
$x0 = random(20, 30);
$k  = $m * $g / $x;
$E  = 0.5 * $k * ($x**2);

BEGIN_PGML
A spring rests at its equilibrium length of [`[$x0]`] cm, with one end hanging from a ceiling. You gently hang a block of mass [`[$m]`] kg onto the end of the spring, the spring stretches beyond its equilibrium length, and you gently bring the spring to rest at its new equilibrium position, where its length is now [`[$x0+100*$x]`] cm.
  
a) Determine the stiffness constant of the spring.

    [_]{$k} [`\textrm{N}/\textrm{m}`]

b) Determine the elastic potential energy stored in the spring.

    [_]{$E} [`\textrm{J}`]
END_PGML

ENDDOCUMENT();
